    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=4ec54663-0058-4347-9828-d4720a7524f4&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201889219221_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "SCHOOL OF EDUCATION COORDINATOR",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-05\n\nLocation: Rochester Hills\n\nJOB TITLE: School of Education Coordinator DEPARTMENT: Education REPORTS TO: Chair, School of Education   COLLEGE MISSION:    Rochester Christian University prepares students for professional and personal success as they serve in God's world.  POSITION SUMMARY:   The School of Education Coordinator is a full-time professional staff position within the Educator Preparation Program (EPP). The Coordinator provides leadership in program compliance, candidate advising, accreditation support, data management, and continuous program improvement. This position serves as the primary coordinator for state certification processes, institutional reporting, and accreditation data systems while maintaining comprehensive candidate records and ensuring compliance with Michigan Department of Education (MDE), Council for the Accreditation of Educator Preparation (CAEP), federal, and university requirements. The Coordinator is responsible for collecting, validating, analyzing, interpreting, and reporting candidate and program data used to evaluate Educator Preparation Program effectiveness, inform departmental decision-making, and support continuous improvement initiatives. The position requires strong analytical skills, attention to detail, and the ability to communicate findings through reports and data dashboards to faculty, administrators, and external agencies. EDUCATION AND/OR EXPERIENCE REQUIRED: Bachelor's degree required; Master's degree preferred Three to five years of experience in higher education, academic advising, student records, institutional reporting, or related administrative work Experience managing confidential student records and databases Experience collecting, analyzing, and reporting data Knowledge of Michigan Teacher Certification requirements preferred Knowledge of Rochester Christian University curriculum and transfer credit evaluation preferred Knowledge of FERPA State Certification and Regulatory Compliance Serve as the School of Education representative to DARTEP and attend quarterly meetings. Maintain current knowledge of Michigan Teacher Certification policies, standards, and legislative changes. Recommend candidates for provisional, renewal, and professional certification through the Michigan Department of Education. Complete all required Michigan Department of Education institutional reports. Complete annual federal Title II reporting. Ensure compliance with all MDE, CAEP, university, and federal reporting requirements. Candidate Records and Academic Advising Maintain complete and accurate candidate records throughout the Educator Preparation Program. Serve as the primary academic advisor for education candidates. Monitor candidate progress toward program completion and certification eligibility. Evaluate transfer transcripts and determine course equivalencies. Update candidate degree plans each semester. Receive and process applications for admission into the Educator Preparation Program. Prepare admission materials for committee review. Conduct iChat criminal background checks for education candidates. Data Collection, Analysis, and Continuous Improvement Collect, organize, validate, analyze, and interpret candidate performance and program effectiveness data. Develop and maintain databases used to monitor candidate progress and program outcomes. Analyze MTTC performance data to identify trends, strengths, areas for improvement, and program effectiveness. Compile candidate assessment data for faculty review and continuous improvement initiatives. Prepare statistical reports, summaries, and dashboards for faculty, administration, accreditation, and state reporting. Monitor key performance indicators related to candidate success, licensure, retention, completion, and employment outcomes. Collect follow-up data from program graduates and employers to evaluate program effectiveness. Assist faculty in using assessment data to support evidence-based program improvements. Maintain data integrity and ensure the accuracy of institutional reporting. Support Quality Assurance processes through systematic collection and analysis of assessment data. Coordinate data collection and documentation required for CAEP accreditation, annual reporting, and accreditation site visits. Generate reports for departmental planning, assessment, strategic initiatives, and institutional effectiveness. Accreditation Support Coordinate collection and organization of evidence required for CAEP accreditation. Maintain accreditation documentation and assessment records. Support faculty in documenting continuous improvement activities. Assist with preparation of accreditation reports, exhibits, and evidence files. Program Operations Manage the Experiential Learning Cloud system for clinical placements and candidate tracking. Maintain the School of Education website. Prepare and revise advising guides, handbooks, manuals, forms, and degree plans. Coordinate departmental records and documentation. Record meeting minutes for School of Education faculty meetings. Order and maintain departmental office supplies and materials. Assist the Dean with course scheduling. Provide required information for administration of the Michigan Future Educator Fellowship and Stipend Grants. Recruitment and Student Support Respond to inquiries from prospective education students. Participate in recruitment events and initiatives. Provide exceptional service and support to prospective and current students, faculty, and external partners. Committee Service Serve as an active member of: Admission Committee Quality Assurance Committee Candidate Support Committee (Chair) Provide data analyses, assessment reports, and recommendations that support committee decision-making and program improvement. Diversity and Institutional Mission Promote an inclusive and welcoming learning environment consistent with the University's mission and diversity statement. Participate in professional development related to diversity, equity, inclusion, and belonging. Support the Christian mission of Rochester Christian University through professional conduct and service. Other Duties Perform other responsibilities as assigned by the Director or Dean of the School of Education. Required Knowledge, Skills, and Abilities Data Analysis and Assessment Demonstrated ability to collect, organize, analyze, interpret, and present quantitative and qualitative data. Strong analytical and critical thinking skills. Ability to identify trends, evaluate program outcomes, and prepare data-informed recommendations. Experience developing reports, statistical summaries, and visual presentations of data. Ability to ensure data accuracy, integrity, confidentiality, and compliance with reporting requirements. Technical Skills Advanced proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, particularly Excel. Proficiency with Google Workspace. Experience using student information systems and education data management software. Ability to learn and effectively utilize the MTTC Analyzer, Canvas, Experiential Learning Cloud, and other assessment management systems. Ability to manage multiple databases simultaneously. Communication and Organizational Skills Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Strong interpersonal skills with the ability to work collaboratively with students, faculty, staff, administrators, and external agencies. Exceptional organizational skills with careful attention to detail. Ability to manage multiple projects while meeting strict reporting deadlines. Ability to maintain confidentiality of sensitive student information. Mission and Professional Expectations Demonstrates support for the mission of Rochester Christian University through evidence of personal faith in Jesus Christ and active participation in a local church. Demonstrates professionalism, integrity, and ethical conduct in all responsibilities.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=4ec54663-0058-4347-9828-d4720a7524f4&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=9201820633066_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "CHAIR - ACCOUNTING & FINANCIAL STUDIES PRGM",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-12\n\nLocation: Rochester Hills\n\nJob Title :       Chair - Accounting and Financial Studies Program Academic Enterprise(s):       School of Business Reports To : Dean - Business and Education University Mission : Rochester Christian University is a Christ-centered institution that empowers students to lead lives of personal and professional significance while serving God in a changing world.   Position Summary : Supports the Dean of Business and Education by providing internal oversight of the Accounting and Financial Studies Program. This includes strategic, curriculum, and budget planning; engaging faculty in program review; and managing personnel, including the hiring, training, and oversight of adjunct instructors. Teaches 21 credit hours per academic year and advises approximately 20 students. Desired Qualifications : It’s preferred that the ideal candidate will have an earned Ph.D. or D.B.A. in an accounting or finance-related discipline from a regionally accredited institution. Candidates with a Master of Accountancy (M.Acc.) or M.B.A. from a regionally accredited institution combined with a CPA license and substantial industry experience will be considered. Record of successful higher education teaching and administrative experience. Three plus years of professional experience in public, corporate, governmental, or non-profit accounting/finance. Previous experience as a department/program chair or director or leading curriculum and accreditation efforts (e.g., ACBSP, AACSB). Experience with modern accounting software, data analytics, or AI-integrated financial tools. The faculty member upholds RCU’s EPIC Academic Philosophy - Exploring, Preparing, Integrating, and Calling - ensuring that even a single-course role reflects the university’s commitment to student formation, Christian mission, and purposeful learning.  EPIC-Aligned Competency Responsibilities : Exploring Intellectual Curiosity, Breadth of Learning, and Study Skills Formation Promotes connections between general education, RCU virtues for the formation of students, and vocation, across the School of Business and the Accounting and Financial Studies Program. Expand contextual understanding and intellectual breadth across programs (liberal arts foundation). Design learning activities that help students explore diverse ways of knowing, problem-solving, and engaging with academic content. Connect course content on study strategies to students’ broader academic goals and intellectual curiosity. Stay current with best practices in college success pedagogy and learning strategies research. Research and pursue grants and outside funding to support the School of Business and the Accounting and Financial Studies Program. Preparing Academic Readiness, Skill-Building, and Professional Competency Manage classes and curriculum, which includes: preparing and presenting changes to the School of Business, assessing program effectiveness, preparing reports for accreditation agencies, adjusting class offerings to match demand, and ensuring efficient scheduling of classes. Facilitate engaging, effective learning activities appropriate for college students in-person and online. Provide knowledge and skill instruction tailored to diverse learners, including adult learners and first-generation college students.  Collaborate with School of Business and Accounting and Financial Studies faculty to ensure consistent delivery and quality across all course sections. Engage faculty in strategic planning, program review, and quality improvement initiatives for majors offered by the program. Use current technology effectively to support instruction and student engagement in both seated and online learning modalities. Integrating Applied Learning, Feedback, and Connection to Campus Resources Represent the department to external stakeholders, including businesses, governments, and nonprofits, and actively seek external partnerships for the School of Business. Act as a brand ambassador for the School of Business and the Accounting and Financial Studies Program. Hold regular program and advisory board meetings. Help students connect academic study skills to real-world application across their disciplines and future careers. Grade assignments and provide timely, constructive feedback that supports student growth and self-awareness. Guide students in accessing campus academic resources and support services as an integral part of the course. Contribute to School of Business team meetings and course alignment discussions to support program coherence and student success outcomes. Evaluate transfer courses within 48 hours or less. Provide academic advising to students, as assigned by the dean or advising services. Calling Faith Integration and Vocational Discernment Uphold and model the university’s Christian mission in all teaching, feedback, and student interactions. Help students connect academic goal-setting and learning strategies to a broader sense of purpose, calling, and service. Create a classroom environment that reflects Christian values of dignity, encouragement, and care for the whole person. Encourage students to view their academic development as part of their formation as persons of faith and vocation. Participate in scholarship and service, and maintain a strong presence on campus as outlined in the faculty handbook. Knowledge, Skills, and Attitudes Ability to manage program budget and personnel, including faculty evaluations and workload, as well as hiring, training, and oversight of adjuncts. Experience and skill in teaching diverse learners, and the ability to use current technology. Ability to manage time independently and to collaborate with campus partners. Ability to communicate clearly and effectively with others. Deep understanding of how students learn, leveraging innovative curriculum and support strategies to drive student success and employability. Insight into industry trends, job market opportunities, and the evolving education landscape. Leadership style that emphasizes collaboration, teamwork, and facilitation. Supportive of the University mission. Indicators of this support include a demonstrated personal faith in Jesus Christ and being active in a local church.  REQUIRED WITH APPLICATION 1. Cover letter - Explicitly highlight your leadership philosophy and explain why your experience fits the specific needs of the Accounting & Financial Studies Program and a Christian university 2. Curriculum Vitae",
      "department": ""
    },
